{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "is_declined",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly encodes the outcome the query centers on (declined vs not), derivable from the original 'state' column. This is the core dependent variable for analyzing declines by category and department."
    },
    {
      "name": "expense_amount_bucket",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Bucketing 'amount' into Low/Medium/High gives a usable axis to check whether declines correlate with expense size. Values are a sound transformation of the numeric amount field; thresholds are unverifiable but reasonable."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Consolidates fine-grained category/description into broad expense types (Hardware, Travel, Software/Services, Other), which is exactly the 'kinds of expenses' grouping the query asks for. Readings from short_description/ci are mostly sound (e.g. Dell Laptop->Hardware, ISP Services->Software/Services), though 'Miscellaneous hardware component'->Other is arguably inconsistent."
    },
    {
      "name": "dept_decline_rate",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A per-department decline rate directly supports the 'does it differ by department' half of the query. However it is a redundant aggregate derivable from is_declined+department, and the row-level values are inconsistent: two rows appear to share a department yet get 4.9, 0.0, and 6.0, and a Software/Services row shows 4.9 while another shows 6.0, suggesting the rate is not a clean, correctly-joined department statistic."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
  "source_groundedness": 0.78,
  "rationale": "The column set targets the query well: is_declined is the outcome, category_type and expense_amount_bucket provide the 'kinds of expenses' axes, and dept_decline_rate addresses the department dimension. Groundedness is generally sound as transformations/categorizations of existing fields, but dept_decline_rate shows internally inconsistent per-row values indicating a flawed derived statistic, and category_type has a minor mis-bucketing. No completeness penalty applied."
}

